mcu: one decode-ladder walk for an aligned word, not two

MCU_Read16 and MCU_Write16 are byte-wise: two full MCU_Read/MCU_Write calls,
each of which re-derives the page and walks the page-0 range ladder. The H8/500
family is word-oriented -- the interpreter's operand loads and stores, and the
stack traffic behind every call and interrupt, come through here -- so the
ladder was being walked twice for almost every memory operand.

A word access is aligned (address &= ~1u is upstream's), so its two bytes
cannot straddle any boundary in the decode. For the regions with no side
effects on read -- rom1, the battery SRAM window at page 0 0x8000..0xdfff,
which lands in sram on every model, and pages 1..4, which mirror rom2 on every
model -- one walk answers both bytes. On the write side only the SRAM window is
side-effect free, so it is the only write arm. Everything else -- device
registers, the PCM and sub-MCU windows, the RAMCR-gated RAM window, unknown
addresses -- falls through to the byte-wise path unchanged.

Each fast-path arm is written as the literal composition of the two byte
accesses it replaces, index arithmetic included, so equivalence does not rest
on the mask being any particular shape.

Proof: patches/tests/mcu/mcu_word_access_equivalence.cpp drives the patched
MCU_Read16/MCU_Write16 and upstream's byte-wise composition over every aligned
address in the 20-bit space, all nine romsets, both RAMCR states and four
rom2_mask shapes, comparing the value, every scalar in mcu_t, every outward
call, and -- for writes -- every byte of every memory array at the end of the
sweep. Its mutants check the test can fail.

--- a/src/backend/mcu.cpp
+++ b/src/backend/mcu.cpp
@@ -639,6 +639,31 @@
 uint16_t MCU_Read16(mcu_t& mcu, uint32_t address)
 {
     address &= ~1u;
+    // A word access is aligned, so its two bytes cannot straddle any boundary
+    // in MCU_Read's decode, and for the regions with no side effects -- rom1,
+    // the battery SRAM window, and the pages that mirror rom2 on every model
+    // -- one walk of the ladder can answer for both bytes instead of two.
+    // Each arm is the literal composition of the two byte reads it replaces;
+    // patches/tests/mcu/mcu_word_access_equivalence.cpp holds it to that over
+    // the whole address space, every romset and every rom2_mask shape.
+    const uint32_t page = (address >> 16) & 0xfu;
+    const uint32_t lo = address & 0xffffu;
+    if (page == 0)
+    {
+        if (!(lo & 0x8000u))
+            return (uint16_t)((mcu.rom1[lo] << 8) + mcu.rom1[lo + 1]);
+        if (lo < 0xe000u)
+        {
+            const uint32_t off = lo & 0x7fffu;
+            return (uint16_t)((mcu.sram[off] << 8) + mcu.sram[off + 1]);
+        }
+    }
+    else if (page <= 4)
+    {
+        const uint32_t hi = mcu.rom2[(address & 0x3ffffu) & mcu.rom2_mask];
+        const uint32_t lo8 = mcu.rom2[((address + 1) & 0x3ffffu) & mcu.rom2_mask];
+        return (uint16_t)((hi << 8) + lo8);
+    }
     uint8_t b0, b1;
     b0 = MCU_Read(mcu, address);
     b1 = MCU_Read(mcu, address+1);
@@ -795,6 +820,20 @@
 void MCU_Write16(mcu_t& mcu, uint32_t address, uint16_t value)
 {
     address &= ~1u;
+    // Same shape as MCU_Read16 above. Only the battery SRAM window is free of
+    // side effects on the write side -- everything else latches, dispatches or
+    // diagnoses -- so it is the only arm.
+    if (((address >> 16) & 0xfu) == 0)
+    {
+        const uint32_t lo = address & 0xffffu;
+        if (lo >= 0x8000u && lo < 0xe000u)
+        {
+            const uint32_t off = lo & 0x7fffu;
+            mcu.sram[off] = (uint8_t)(value >> 8);
+            mcu.sram[off + 1] = (uint8_t)(value & 0xff);
+            return;
+        }
+    }
     MCU_Write(mcu, address, (uint8_t)(value >> 8));
     MCU_Write(mcu, address + 1, (uint8_t)(value & 0xff));
 }
